    <item>
      <title>“God is with us”: The Voice of Hind Rajab</title>
      <link>https://www.aprilonline.org/god-is-with-us-the-voice-of-hind-rajab/?utm_source=rss&amp;utm_medium=rss&amp;utm_campaign=god-is-with-us-the-voice-of-hind-rajab</link>
      <description>Kristian Petersen reviews The Voice of Hind Rajab, Kaouther Ben Hania’s Oscar-nominated film, focusing on its portrayal of faith, grief, and Palestinian suffering through the tragic story of five-year-old Hind Rajab. The essay shows how religion, especially the invocation “God is with us,” becomes a source of consolation, endurance, and moral witness in the face of devastating violence.</description>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.aprilonline.org/god-is-with-us-the-voice-of-hind-rajab/?utm_source=rss&amp;utm_medium=rss&amp;utm_campaign=god-is-with-us-the-voice-of-hind-rajab</guid>
      <enclosure url="https://www.aprilonline.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Screenshot-2026-03-11-at-9.16.54-PM-scaled.png" length="0" type="image/png"/>
      <pubDate>Wed, 15 Apr 2026 06:41:17 +0000</pubDate>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>In the Garden: Forevergreen</title>
      <link>https://www.aprilonline.org/in-the-garden-forevergreen/?utm_source=rss&amp;utm_medium=rss&amp;utm_campaign=in-the-garden-forevergreen</link>
      <description>Anthony D. Padgett’s review of Forevergreen explores the animated short’s rich religious symbolism, reading the film through Genesis, Eden, sacrifice, and the Tree of Life. He highlights how Forevergreen connects interspecies love, ecological loss, and spiritual themes while reflecting on humanity’s relationship to nature, consumption, and redemption.</description>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.aprilonline.org/in-the-garden-forevergreen/?utm_source=rss&amp;utm_medium=rss&amp;utm_campaign=in-the-garden-forevergreen</guid>
